{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["And the reason for <b>וּפוֹתֵר אֵין אוֹתוֹ</b> ('but there is no one who interprets it') — there is no one who informs the future from it. And it is possible that they sent for some of the magicians in the morning, or that there were men with them in the prison house and there was no one interpreting it, or that they said: there is no one in the world who can interpret it according to our understanding, for it is very obscure:" "<b>הֲלוֹא לֶאֱלֹהִים פִּתְרֹנִים</b> ('Do not interpretations belong to Elohim?'). Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 40:8) explained that he means to say: the events that are destined to come, which appear in dreams — they belong to Elohim, for He alone brings them about and informs the future, and He is the one who makes peace and creates evil, and there is in my words to you neither benefit nor loss. And he said this so that they would not punish him if evil were to come upon them, or so that they would recount them to him and not despise him. But if so, the word 'הֲלוֹא' ('is it not?') has no meaning in this context. Perhaps its meaning is like 'הִנֵּה' ('behold') — he says: behold, to Elohim alone belong interpretations, and not to the man who interprets. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he said: 'Do not interpretations belong to Elohim' — concerning all obscure and sealed dreams, and He is able to inform the interpretation of your dream. And if it is obscure in your eyes, tell it to me now — perhaps it will be pleasing in His eyes to reveal His secret to me.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:20.235332921Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}